be I'm missing a shorter path. Let me see if there's a direct connection from Faiga to someone else. Faiga is friends with Nevaeh and T. From T, we can go to K, who is connected to Nevaeh. So the path is Faiga -> T -> K -> Nevaeh. That's three steps. Alternatively, Faiga -> Nevaeh -> K -> T. Also three steps. So both paths are equally long.\n\nWait, but maybe there's a way to get to K faster. Let me check the connections again. From Faiga, if she sends to Nevaeh, Nevaeh is connected to K. K is connected to T. T is connected to F. So Faiga -> Nevaeh -> K -> T. That's three steps. Alternatively, if she sends to T, T is connected to K, who is connected to Nevaeh, so Faiga -> T -> K -> Nevaeh. Also three steps. So both paths are the same length.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's a way to get to K in two steps. Let me see. From Faiga, she can go to Nevaeh, who is connected to K. So Faiga -> Nevaeh -> K. That's two steps. Then K can pass to T, but that's an extra step. So the shortest path would be Faiga -> Nevaeh -> K -> T. But that's three steps. Alternatively, if she sends to T, T can go to K, who can go to Nevaeh. So Faiga -> T -> K -> Nevaeh. Also three steps.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating this. The question is whether Faiga should pass to T or Nevaeh first. From Faiga, both T and Nevaeh are directly connected. From T, we can reach K, who can reach Nevaeh. From Nevaeh, we can reach K, who can reach T. So the shortest path would be either way, but perhaps passing to T first allows reaching K and then Nevaeh in two steps, making the total path Faiga -> T -> K -> Nevaeh, which is three steps. Alternatively, Faiga -> Nevaeh -> K -> T, which is also three steps.\n\nWait, but maybe there's a shorter path. Let me check if there's a connection from T to Nevaeh directly. From the list, T is friends with K and F. K is friends with Nevaeh, so T can reach Nevaeh via K. So the path is T -> K -> Nevaeh. So if Faiga sends to T, then T can pass to K, who can pass to Nevaeh. So the pa